<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John IV of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1604">1604</a>–<a href="/years/1656">1656</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Duke of Braganza, John the Restorer · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza">House of Braganza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John IV of Portugal, the eighth Duke of Braganza, was proclaimed king on 1 December 1640 when Portuguese nobles rose against Spanish rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. Founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza">Braganza dynasty</a>, he fought the Restoration War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. Spain recognised Portuguese independence in 1668, after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal.jpg" alt="John IV of Portugal" width="320" height="493" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_John,_Duke_of_Braganza_c._1630_(The_Royal_Castle_in_Warsaw).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A revolt on 1 December 1640 placed the <b>Duke of Braganza</b> on the throne as John IV, and Spain recognised independence by the Treaty of Lisbon in 1668.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the restoration of independence under <b>João IV</b> in 1640, Brazil became the empire’s centre of gravity, its sugar economy sustaining Portugal through the seventeenth century until the Brazilian Gold Rush, sparked by the discovery of gold in Minas Gerais in the 1690s, and the discovery of diamonds…</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Nobles stormed the royal palace in Lisbon and proclaimed the <b>Duke of Braganza</b> king as John IV.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 December 1640 Portuguese nobles rose in Lisbon and proclaimed the <b>Duke of Braganza</b> as John IV.</blockquote><a href="/state/iberian-union">Iberian Union</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ducal Palace of Vila Viçosa, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Ribeira Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.78,-7.42&amp;year=1637" aria-label="Ducal Palace of Vila Viçosa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4366 772 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John IV of Portugal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4816 1042h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4816 1042h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.78,-7.42&amp;year=1637">What was here in 1637? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
